-//! The find bar's "In selection" option: count, replace all and highlight only inside a range
-//! of the text, the selection the option was turned on with (or that the find bar opened with,
-//! when it is long, see [`auto`]). As in Notepad++, Find next, Find previous and Replace keep
-//! working on the whole text: the match they select leaves the range as it is.
+//! The find bar's "In selection" option: count, replace all and highlight only inside the
+//! selection, as in Notepad++. The option is a ticked box, enabled while there is a selection;
+//! the range is the selection at the time of the action, never one stored from earlier. Find
+//! next, Find previous and Replace keep working on the whole text.
 //!
-//! The range is two positions, (line, byte index), start before end. A match counts when it
-//! lies wholly inside. Replace and Replace all change the text, so the range moves with their
-//! edits ([`shift`], [`edited`]); any other edit (or another tab) makes the range stale (the
-//! caller compares text generations), as it would no longer cover what was selected. A stale
-//! range gives way to the selection at the time of the action, as Notepad++ takes it; with
-//! nothing selected the option shows off and disabled, and the actions take the whole text
-//! ([`scope`]).
+//! A range is two positions, (line, byte index), start before end. A match counts when it lies
+//! wholly inside. Replace all moves the end of the range by its edits ([`shift`]).
 
 use crate::find_count::{Matches, Span};
 
